"National conventions | USAGov (https://www.usa.gov/national-conventions)\nciteturn28view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.usa.gov/national-conventions\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 102\nL0: 【0†GTM†www.googletagmanager.com】 【1†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【2†Image: Close】\nL3: \nL4:   * 【3†All topics and services】\nL5:   * 【4†The U.S. and its government】\nL6:   * 【5†Government benefits】\nL7:   * 【6†Immigration and U.S. citizenship】\nL8:   * 【7†Money and credit】\nL9:   * 【8†Travel】\nL10:   * 【9†Voting and elections】\nL11:   * 【9†Voting and elections】\nL12:     * 【10†Voter registration】\nL13:     * 【11†How, when, and where to vote】\nL14:     * 【12†Decide who to vote for】\nL15:     * 【13†Find my state or local election office】\nL16:     * 【14†Congressional, state, and local elections】\nL17:     * 【15†Voting and election laws】\nL18:     * 【16†How the president is elected】\nL19:       * 【17†Overview of the presidential election process】\nL20:       * 【18†Requirements for presidential candidates】\nL21:       * 【19†Electoral College】\nL22:       * current page\nL23: \nL24: National conventions\nL25:       * 【20†Primaries and caucuses】\nL26:       * 【21†Presidential general election】\nL27:     * 【22†Inauguration of the president】\nL28:     * 【23†Results of past elections】\nL29: # National conventions\nL30: \nL31: Political parties hold national conventions to select presidential and vice presidential nominees. Learn how the national conventions work.\nL32: ## What happens at a national political convention?\nL33: \nL34: To become the presidential nominee, a candidate typically has to win a majority of delegates. This usually happens through the party's state primaries and caucuses. 【20†Learn more about state presidential primaries and caucuses.】 This includes how delegates are chosen for the national convention.\nL35:   * State delegates go to the national convention to confirm their choice of candidates by casting votes.\nL36:     * It is possible that no candidate gets the majority of a party's delegates during the primaries and caucuses. This results in convention delegates choosing the nominee through extra rounds of voting.\nL37: \nL38: At the convention, the presidential nominee officially announces their choice for vice president.\nL39: ## Types of delegates at a national convention\nL40: \nL41: There are two main types of delegates.\nL42: \nL43:   1. Pledged or bound delegates must support the candidate they were awarded in the primary or caucus.\nL44:   2. Unpledged delegates or superdelegates can support any presidential candidate they choose.\nL45: ## Contested and brokered conventions\nL46: \nL47: In rare cases, none of the party's candidates may have a majority of delegates going into the convention. The convention is then considered \"contested.\" Delegates will pick their presidential nominee through one or more rounds of voting.\nL48: \nL49: In the first round of voting:\nL50:   * Pledged delegates usually have to vote for the candidate they were awarded to at the start of the convention.\nL51:   * Unpledged delegates can vote for any candidate.\nL52:   * Superdelegates in the Democratic Party cannot vote in the first round of a contested convention. But they can vote in the first round of a convention if a candidate already has secured enough delegates through the primaries and caucuses to get the nomination.\nL53: In the rare instance that no nominee wins in the first round, the convention is then considered \"brokered.\"\nL54: \nL55:   * The pledged delegates may choose any candidate in later rounds of voting.\nL56:   * Superdelegates can also vote in these later rounds.\nL57: \nL58: Balloting continues until one candidate receives the required majority to win the nomination.\nL59: \nL60: LAST UPDATED: February 25, 2026\nL61: \nL62: SHARE THIS PAGE:\nL63: ## Have a question?\nL64: \nL65: Ask a real person any government-related question for free. They will get you the answer or let you know where to find it.\nL66: \nL67: 【24†Call USAGov】\nL68: \nL69: 【25†Chat with USAGov】\nL70: \nL71: 【26†Top】\nL72: \nL73: ### Government information\nL74: \nL75:   * 【3†All topics and services】\nL76:   * 【27†Directory of U.S. government agencies and departments】\nL77:   * 【28†Branches of government】\nL78: \nL79: ### About us\nL80: \nL81:   * 【29†About USAGov】\nL82:   * 【30†Report a website issue】\nL83:   * 【31†Website usage data】\nL84: ### For federal agencies\nL85: \nL86:   * 【32†Partner with us】\nL87:   * 【33†Read our blog】\nL88: #### USAGov Contact Center\nL89: \nL90: Ask USA.gov a question at\nL91: \nL92: 【24†1-844-USAGOV1 (1-844-872-4681)】\nL93: \nL94: USAGov is the official guide to government information and services\nL95: \nL96: An official website of the 【34†U.S. General Services Administration†www.gsa.gov】\nL97: \nL98: This website complies with Executive Order 【35†14224†www.federalregister.gov】. English is the official language of the U.S. Government, and the English-language version of this website is the authoritative source for all federal information contained herein.\nL99:   * 【36†Accessibility policy】\nL100:   * 【37†Privacy and security policies】\nL101:   * 【38†FOIA requests†www.gsa.gov】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPresidential primaries and caucuses | USAGov (https://www.usa.gov/primaries-caucuses)\nciteturn28view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.usa.gov/primaries-caucuses\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 114\nL0: 【0†GTM†www.googletagmanager.com】 【1†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【2†Image: Close】\nL3: \nL4:   * 【3†All topics and services】\nL5:   * 【4†The U.S. and its government】\nL6:   * 【5†Government benefits】\nL7:   * 【6†Immigration and U.S. citizenship】\nL8:   * 【7†Money and credit】\nL9:   * 【8†Travel】\nL10:   * 【9†Voting and elections】\nL11:   * 【9†Voting and elections】\nL12:     * 【10†Voter registration】\nL13:     * 【11†How, when, and where to vote】\nL14:     * 【12†Decide who to vote for】\nL15:     * 【13†Find my state or local election office】\nL16:     * 【14†Congressional, state, and local elections】\nL17:     * 【15†Voting and election laws】\nL18:     * 【16†How the president is elected】\nL19:       * 【17†Overview of the presidential election process】\nL20:       * 【18†Requirements for presidential candidates】\nL21:       * 【19†Electoral College】\nL22:       * 【20†National conventions】\nL23:       * current page\nL24: Primaries and caucuses\nL25: \nL26:       * 【21†Presidential general election】\nL27:     * 【22†Inauguration of the president】\nL28:     * 【23†Results of past elections】\nL29: # Presidential primaries and caucuses\nL30: \nL31: Primaries and caucuses are two ways that people help states and political parties choose presidential nominees. Learn how they work and the differences between the processes.\nL32: \nL33: ## The difference between primaries and caucuses and how they work\nL34: ### Presidential primaries\nL35: \nL36: Most states hold primaries 6-9 months before a presidential election. Primary voters choose their preferred candidate anonymously by casting secret ballots. The state where the primary is held takes the results of the vote into account to award delegates to the winners.\nL37: ### Caucuses\nL38: \nL39: Several states hold caucuses in the months leading up to a presidential election. Caucuses are meetings run by political parties that are held at the county, district, or precinct level.\nL40:   * Some caucuses choose candidates by secret ballot.\nL41:   * Others require participants to form groups according to the candidate they support. Undecided participants form their own group. Each candidate’s group gives speeches and tries to get others to join their group. At the end, the number of delegates given to each candidate is based on the number of caucus votes they received.\nL42: ### Types of primaries and caucuses\nL43: \nL44: Depending on state and political party rules, primaries and caucuses can be:\nL45: \nL46:   * Open\nL47:   * Closed\nL48:   * A combination of both\nL49: \nL50: During an open primary or caucus, voters do not have to be registered with a political party to take part in its primary or caucus.\nL51: \nL52: During a closed primary or caucus, only voters registered with that party can take part and vote.\nL53: \nL54: \"Semi-open\" and \"semi-closed\" primaries and caucuses are variations of the two main types.\nL55: Each state has its own way of operating its primaries and caucuses. 【24†Learn which states have which types of primaries.†www.ncsl.org】\nL56: ## Awarding delegates from the primaries and caucuses\nL57: \nL58: The number of delegates awarded to each candidate in a primary or caucus is based on a complex set of rules. These rules vary by state, and are set by the political parties at both a national and state level.\nL59: \nL60: In most cases, the people chosen to be delegates are:\nL61: \nL62:   * Active party members\nL63:   * Leaders\nL64:   * Early supporters of one of the candidates\nL65: \nL66: For information about your state's presidential primaries or caucuses, contact:\nL67:   * 【13†Your state or local election office】\nL68:   * The political party of your choice.\nL69: \nL70: Once delegates are selected, they go on to represent their state at national party conventions. 【20†Learn about the role of delegates in choosing their party’s presidential nominee】 during the national conventions.\nL71: \nL72: LAST UPDATED: February 25, 2026\nL73: \nL74: SHARE THIS PAGE:\nL75: ## Have a question?\nL76: \nL77: Ask a real person any government-related question for free. They will get you the answer or let you know where to find it.\nL78: \nL79: 【25†Call USAGov】\nL80: \nL81: 【26†Chat with USAGov】\nL82: \nL83: 【27†Top】\nL84: \nL85: ### Government information\nL86: \nL87:   * 【3†All topics and services】\nL88:   * 【28†Directory of U.S. government agencies and departments】\nL89:   * 【29†Branches of government】\nL90: \nL91: ### About us\nL92: \nL93:   * 【30†About USAGov】\nL94:   * 【31†Report a website issue】\nL95:   * 【32†Website usage data】\nL96: ### For federal agencies\nL97: \nL98:   * 【33†Partner with us】\nL99:   * 【34†Read our blog】\nL100: #### USAGov Contact Center\nL101: \nL102: Ask USA.gov a question at\nL103: \nL104: 【25†1-844-USAGOV1 (1-844-872-4681)】\nL105: \nL106: USAGov is the official guide to government information and services\nL107: \nL108: An official website of the 【35†U.S. General Services Administration†www.gsa.gov】\nL109: \nL110: This website complies with Executive Order 【36†14224†www.federalregister.gov】. English is the official language of the U.S. Government, and the English-language version of this website is the authoritative source for all federal information contained herein.\nL111:   * 【37†Accessibility policy】\nL112:   * 【38†Privacy and security policies】\nL113:   * 【39†FOIA requests†www.gsa.gov】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSalisbury Doctrine - UK Parliament (https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/salisbury-doctrine/)\nciteturn28view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/salisbury-doctrine/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 224\nL0: 【0††www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Skip to main content】 【2†Menu】\nL3: \nL4: Menu\nL5: \nL6: Select an area to explore\nL7: \nL8:   * 【2†Business】\nL9:   * 【2†MPs, Lords & offices】\nL10:   * 【2†About】\nL11:   * 【2†Get involved】\nL12:   * 【2†Visit】\nL13:   * 【2†Learning】\nL14:   * 【3†Shop†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL15:   * 【2†More】\nL16: \nL17:   * Or type a keyword to search\nL18: \nL19: [Input: Search UK Parliament]\nL20: \nL21: [Button: Search]\nL22: \nL23: 【2†Close】\nL24: \nL25: [Input]\nL26: \nL27: 【2†Close】\nL28: ## Parliamentary business\nL29: \nL30: Find out what’s on today at the House of Commons and House of Lords. Track current bills, keep up with committees, watch live footage and follow topical issues.\nL31: \nL32: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL33: ### In this section\nL34: \nL35:   * 【4†Parliamentary business home】\nL36:   * 【5†House of Commons】\nL37:   * 【6†House of Lords】\nL38:   * 【7†What's on†calendar.parliament.uk】\nL39:   * 【8†Bills & legislation】\nL40:   * 【9†Committees†committees.parliament.uk】\nL41:   * 【10†Publications & records】\nL42:   * 【11†Parliament TV†www.parliamentlive.tv】\nL43:   * 【12†News from UK Parliament】\nL44: ### Legislation and debate\nL45: \nL46: 【13†Hansard Read transcripts of debates in both Houses.†hansard.parliament.uk】 【14†Research briefings Produced by Commons Library, Lords Library, and Parliamentary Office Science and Technology.†researchbriefings.parliament.uk】\nL47: ## MPs, Lords & offices\nL48: \nL49: MPs and Members of the Lords sit in the two Chambers of Parliament scrutinising the Government and debating legislation. Find Members of Parliament (MPs) by postcode and constituency, and Members of the House of Lords by name and party.\nL50: \nL51: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL52: ### In this section\nL53: \nL54:   * 【15†MPs, Lords & offices home】\nL55:   * 【16†MPs†members.parliament.uk】\nL56:   * 【17†Members of the House of Lords†members.parliament.uk】\nL57:   * 【18†Government & opposition†members.parliament.uk】\nL58:   * 【19†Parliamentary offices】\nL59:   * 【20†Members' allowances】\nL60:   * 【21†Standards and financial interests】\nL61: ### Members of Parliament\nL62: \nL63: 【22†Find your MP Search for Members by name, postcode, or constituency†members.parliament.uk】 【17†Search for Members of the House of Lords Learn about their experience, knowledge and interests.†members.parliament.uk】\nL64: \nL65: ## About Parliament\nL66: \nL67: Parliament examines what the Government is doing, makes new laws, holds the power to set taxes and debates the issues of the day.\nL68: \nL69: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL70: ### In this section\nL71: \nL72:   * 【23†About Parliament home】\nL73:   * 【24†How Parliament works】\nL74:   * 【25†About MPs, Lords & officers】\nL75:   * 【26†Independent Complaints and Grievance Scheme (ICGS)】\nL76:   * 【27†Living Heritage】\nL77:   * 【28†Careers at Parliament】\nL78:   * 【29†Frequently Asked Questions】\nL79:   * 【30†Sustainability and environmental performance in Parliament】\nL80:   * 【31†UK Parliament's Modern Slavery Programme】\nL81: ## Get involved\nL82: \nL83: Read about how to contact an MP or Lord, petition Parliament and find out details of events in your area.\nL84: \nL85: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL86: \nL87: ### In this section\nL88: \nL89:   * 【32†Get involved home】\nL90:   * 【33†Contact an MP or a Member of the Lords】\nL91:   * 【34†Join a learning programme】\nL92:   * 【35†Research impact at the UK Parliament】\nL93:   * 【36†Have your say: Laws and debates】\nL94:   * 【37†Y Gornel Gymraeg】\nL95: ### Get involved with Parliament\nL96: \nL97: 【33†Contact an MP or Lord Contact your MP or a Member of the House of Lords about an issue that matters to you.】 【38†Keep up to date Sign up for the Your Parliament newsletter to find out how you can get involved.†parliament.us16.list-manage.com】\nL98: \nL99: ### It starts with action\nL100: \nL101:   * 【39†Sign or create a petition】\nL102:   * 【40†Sign up to UK Parliament Week†www.ukparliamentweek.org】\nL103: ## Visit Parliament\nL104: \nL105: Find out how to take a tour in person, join an online event, book tickets and plan a visit to UK Parliament.\nL106: \nL107: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL108: \nL109: ### In this section\nL110: \nL111:   * 【41†Visit Parliament home】\nL112:   * 【42†Tours】\nL113:   * 【43†Talks and events】\nL114:   * 【44†Watch debates and committees】\nL115:   * 【45†Plan your visit】\nL116:   * 【46†360° virtual tour】\nL117:   * 【47†Venue hire】\nL118:   * 【3†Parliament Shop†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL119: ### Keep up to date\nL120: \nL121: 【48†Visit newsletter Sign up to our newsletter to receive the latest news about visiting UK Parliament.†parliament.us14.list-manage.com】\nL122: \nL123: ## Learning\nL124: \nL125: Your UK Parliament offers free, flexible support for teachers, community groups, and home educators to spark engagement and active citizenship.\nL126: \nL127: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL128: ### In this section\nL129: \nL130:   * 【49†Learning home†learning.parliament.uk】\nL131:   * 【50†About UK Parliament education†learning.parliament.uk】\nL132:   * 【51†Teacher resources and lesson plans†learning.parliament.uk】\nL133:   * 【52†CPD Training†learning.parliament.uk】\nL134:   * 【53†Outreach†learning.parliament.uk】\nL135:   * 【54†Visit†learning.parliament.uk】\nL136: ### Supporting teachers\nL137: \nL138: 【54†Inspire your students Book a school visit, classroom workshop or teacher-training session†learning.parliament.uk】 【55†Free teacher training resources Access videos, worksheets, lesson plans and games.†learning.parliament.uk】\nL139: \nL140: ### Keep up to date\nL141: \nL142:   * 【56†Sign up to our education newsletter†parliament.us16.list-manage.com】\nL143: \nL144: ## Shop\nL145: \nL146: Shop\nL147: \nL148: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL149: \nL150: ### In this section\nL151: \nL152:   * 【3†Shop home†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL153: \nL154: 【57†UK Parliament】\nL155: # Salisbury Doctrine\nL156: \nL157:   1. 【58†All】\nL158:   2. 【59†A】\nL159:   3. 【60†B】\nL160:   4. 【61†C】\nL161:   5. 【62†D】\nL162:   6. 【63†E】\nL163:   7. 【64†F】\nL164:   8. 【65†G】\nL165:   9. 【66†H】\nL166:   10. 【67†I】\nL167:   11. 【68†J】\nL168:   12. 【69†K】\nL169:   13. 【70†L】\nL170:   14. 【71†M】\nL171:   15. 【72†N】\nL172:   16. 【73†O】\nL173:   17. 【74†P】\nL174:   18. 【75†Q】\nL175:   19. 【76†R】\nL176:   20. 【77†S】\nL177:   21. 【78†T】\nL178:   22. 【79†U】\nL179:   23. 【80†V】\nL180:   24. 【81†W】\nL181:   25. 【82†X】\nL182:   26. 【83†Y】\nL183:   27. 【84†Z】\nL184: The Salisbury Doctrine, or \"Convention\" as it is sometimes called, emerged from the working arrangements reached during the Labour Government of 1945-51, when the fifth Marquess of Salisbury was the Leader of the Conservative Opposition in the Lords. The Convention ensures that major Government Bills can get through the Lords when the Government of the day has no majority in the Lords.\nL185: In practice, it means that the Lords does not try to vote down at second or third reading, a Government Bill mentioned in an election manifesto.\nL186:   * 【85†Research briefing: The Salisbury Doctrine】\nL187:   * 【86†Related glossary term: Conventions】\nL188: ###### Popular\nL189: \nL190:   * 【87†What's on in Parliament†whatson.parliament.uk】\nL191:   * 【88†Petitions†petition.parliament.uk】\nL192:   * 【28†Careers at Parliament】\nL193:   * 【89†Commons Media Relations】\nL194:   * 【90†Lords Press Office】\nL195:   * 【91†Contact us】\nL196: \nL197: ###### Research\nL198: \nL199:   * 【92†Commons Library†commonslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL200:   * 【93†Lords Library†lordslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL201:   * 【94†POST†post.parliament.uk】\nL202: \nL203: ###### Search Parliament\nL204: \nL205: [Input]\nL206: \nL207: [Input]\nL208: \nL209: ###### Watch Parliament Live\nL210: \nL211: 【95†Parliament Live TV†parliamentlive.tv】\nL212: ###### Follow Parliament\nL213: \nL214: ###### Legal\nL215: \nL216:   * 【96†Accessibility statement】\nL217:   * 【31†Modern slavery statement】\nL218:   * 【97†About this site】\nL219:   * 【98†Privacy notice】\nL220:   * 【99†Cookie policy】\nL221:   * 【2†Cookie settings】\nL222: \nL223: © UK Parliament 2026\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Speaker of the House of Commons - UK Parliament (https://www.parliament.uk/business/commons/the-speaker/)\nciteturn28view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn27view0\",\"id\":60}); Total lines: 273\nL0: 【0††www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Skip to main content】 【2†Menu】\nL3: \nL4: Menu\nL5: \nL6: Select an area